**Read:** [Introduction to Mineral Processing — Arun Kumar Majumder, NPTEL](../sources/Introduction-to-Mineral-Processing-NPTEL.lingua#r_5VQ67PA1160RQ1EWN17211XBWD), the entire iron-ore washing sequence and the earlier particle/fluid-motion material; [Tratamento de Minérios — CETEM, sixth edition](../sources/Tratamento-de-Minerios-CETEM.lingua#r_04ZQFADZ3NC3T6QDPQ565W1ERA), complete applicable washing, classification, and water-treatment discussions; [Tratamento de Minérios: Práticas Laboratoriais — Sampaio, França, and Braga](../sources/CETEM-Praticas-Laboratoriais.lingua#r_44AAWXB1D7MBR9B0V3TYB8GF9B), the full preparation and size/separation methods used.

Washing, scrubbing, attrition, and desliming alter surface coatings, aggregates, clay associations, and size distributions. They are not synonymous, and fine material is not automatically waste. Valuable minerals, impurities, adsorbed species, and process-water constituents can follow the removed fraction.

Describe the feed state, proposed removal mechanism, apparatus, water/energy duty, and resulting populations. Compare liberation of a coating or weak aggregate with breakage of useful particles. Slurry viscosity, settling behavior, chemical environment, and later separation response can change after a seemingly simple preparation stage.

Close solids, valuable/deleterious constituents, and water through washed product, fines, returns, and any treatment system. Evaluate quality requirements on their actual wet/dry and particle-size bases. A decrease in one impurity percentage may come partly from rejecting valuable mass or changing the denominator.

**Work:** use a complete iron-ore or industrial-mineral washing case. Reconstruct size-by-size and constituent-by-constituent balances, compare with an alternative preparation route, and account for the generated fine slurry and water reuse. Explain which measurements establish actual coating removal and which only show a bulk composition change. Design a representative feed campaign that includes the troublesome material domains.

**Continue:** [Connect thermal preparation with reaction, phase change, heat, and product quality](Calcination-transformations-heat-and-processing-boundaries.lingua#r_13PQDG6YPAFH3MW1DABBX0G2YG).

